ound are not part of the scene.\n<Subject 3> is the palace corridor and banquet-hall entrance defined by <Picture 3>: tall arched windows, cream stone columns and vaults, pale stone flooring, dark marble walls, carved double doors, and a chandelier and a few guests visible inside. Its architecture, materials, and daylight guide the setting; the reference image’s empty wide composition is not reproduced.\n\nsummary:\n[reference generation] A 15-second, 16:9 live-action royal romance scene places <Subject 1> and <Subject 2> outside the banquet hall in <Subject 3>. Their quiet confrontation reveals why their engagement ended. When he notices that she still wears their engagement ring, she stops at the door without entering.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4], [Shot 5], [Shot 6]): fully_preserved - preserve his facial identity, silver-white hair, physique, black clothing, muted-gold coat embroidery, trousers, and boots.\n<Subject 2> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4], [Shot 5], [Shot 6]): fully_preserved - preserve her facial identity, light-blonde updo, black feather adornments, gown, draped fabric, and consistently pale, naturally textured skin.\n<Subject 3> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3], [Shot 4], [Shot 5], [Shot 6]): fully_preserved - retain the corridor’s architecture and materials, banquet-hall doorway, and the direction of the daylight while choosing closer camera positions for the action.\n\ndetailed_description:\nRealistic live action in clear daytime light. The arched windows provide the principal light from one consistent side of the corridor; pale stone and the doorframe softly reflect it into the shadows. Faces retain natural skin texture and gentle tonal transitions. Black feathers, fabric, and muted-gold embroidery remain legible. No film grain, added noise, heavy skin smoothing, digital sharpening, orange key light, or jewelry glow. Both characters speak quietly enough to avoid drawing attention from the banquet. The camera stays at chest height or closer, never cutting to a corridor-wide view or an isolated ring close-up.\n\n[Shot 1] A slightly oblique two-person medium close shot outside the carved doors of <Subject 3>. <Subject 2>, wearing the black feathered gown, has been heading toward the banquet hall; <Subject 1>, in the embroidered black coat, comes alongside her and she stops. They are about one step apart. He remains on the corridor side, leaving her route to the door open. Window light reaches the side of each person facing the windows. He checks that she is listening, then <Subject 1> (S1) asks in a low, controlled voice, <d>[English] Did you ask them to end it?</d> He watches for a denial. She starts to answer, sees that he is waiting for the truth, and hesitates.\n\n[Shot 2] At 00:02.400, cut to a close shot of <Subject 2> (S2), with only the edge of his shoulder in frame. She briefly checks whether anyone beyond the doorway can hear, then returns her gaze to him. A short, gentle camera move begins after she starts speaking and stops when she finishes. She says, quietly but clearly, <d>[English] They would have ruined your family.</d> Her aim is to make him understand the danger. She studies his response; he understands, yet remains where he is. The same window light shapes one side of her face, while pale stone provides soft reflected light on the other.\n\n[Shot 3] At 00:05.100, cut to a close shot of <Subject 1> (S1). His lighting follows his established position in the same corridor. He appears ready to ask about the threat, then notices that she is waiting for him to accept her decision. Without stepping closer, he changes course and says, <d>[English] You should have let me choose.</d> The hurt lies in being denied a choice, not in a raised voice. He stays still and gives her room to answer.\n\n[Shot 4] At 00:07.900, cut back to <Subject 2> (S2). She starts to form another explanation, realizes it would again speak on his behalf, and lets it go. Looking at him, she says, <d>[English] I couldn't.</d> She waits for him to respond; he does not. She breaks eye contact and turns toward the banquet-hall doors. The camera makes a small lateral adjustment to keep her face readable, then follows her continuous movement. As she turns, window light shifts naturally from her cheek toward her hair and shoulder; the pale doorframe and stone wall softly illuminate her face. Her loose strands, feather adornments, and draped fabric move with the turn. <Subject 1> stays in place.\n\n[Shot 5] At 00:10.100, continue following her toward the carved door in a medium close shot, keeping her face, shoulder, and left arm in frame. On the ring finger of her left hand is one intact royal engagement ring: an elegant, modestly sized oval pale center diamond, fine diamond halo, and platinum band engraved with a family crest. She raises that hand and touches the door handle. The ring enters the composition as a natural result of the action; a narrow line of window light briefly makes its stone and setting readable, without a separate insert or artificial sparkle. Her hand remains the same pale skin tone as her face, neck, shoulder, and arm, with only the expected change in brightness from its angle to the window. <Subject 1> first sees the ring, then looks at her face. <Subject 1> (S1) asks softly, without triumph, <d>[English] Then why are you still wearing my ring?</d> Her hand remains on the handle. She does not immediately turn.\n\n[Shot 6] At 00:12.600, hold a medium close composition that includes <Subject 2>’s face, left hand, ring, and part of the doorframe. A very short, slow camera move forward begins while her hand is on the handle and stops as she releases it. She looks toward the banquet inside, but does not push the door. After a beat, her fingers loosen from the handle and she turns back to find <Subject 1>, who has remained in his original position and meets her gaze. She stays outside the threshold. Neither approaches, embraces, or makes another promise. End on the unresolved look between them at 00:15.000.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nLow, indistinct conversation and occasional soft glassware sounds come from inside the banquet hall. Close sounds include restrained breathing, small footsteps, the movement of her gown and draped fabric, and her hand touching and releasing the door handle. Only the five specified lines are spoken, by their identified speakers, with accurate lip synchronization. No narration, subtitles, or additional intelligible dialogue.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nN/A"], "inputs": [{"widget": {"name": "value"}, "name": "value", "label": "value", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "value"}], "flags": {}, "type": "PrimitiveStringMultiline", "title": "提示词Input Text (Prompt)", "mode": 0, "bgcolor": "#353", "size": [630.290771484375, 462.6627502441406], "pos": [-2037.6678466796875, 6016.58251953125], "id": 217, "properties": {"cnr_id": "comfy-core", "ver": "0.30.0", "widget_ue_connectable": {}, "Node name for S&R": "PrimitiveStringMultiline"}, "order": 11, "widgets_values_named": {"value": "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1> is the male lead defined by <Picture 1>: the same face, silver-white medium-length hair, build, black shirt, black long coat with muted-gold embroidery, dark trousers, and leather boots. The character-sheet layout and studio background are not part of the scene.\n<Subject 2> is the female lead defined by <Picture 2>: the same face, pale natural complexion, light-blonde updo, black feather hair adornments, black feathered evening gown, and black fabric draped over her arms. The character-sheet layout and studio background are not part of the scene.\n<Subject 3> is the palace corridor and banquet-hall entrance defined by <Picture 3>: tall arched windows, cream stone columns and vaults, pale stone flooring, dark marble walls, carved double doors, and a chandelier and a few guests visible inside. Its architecture, materials, and da